      "options": {
        "content": "| Variable | Value now |\n|---|---|\n| `$​category` | ${category} |\n| `$​valueMin` / `$​valueMax` | ${valueMin} / ${valueMax} |\n| `$​site` | ${site} |\n| `$​lat` / `$​lng` | ${lat} / ${lng} |\n| `$​west` / `$​south` / `$​east` / `$​north` | ${west} / ${south} / ${east} / ${north} |\n\n**Filters — two ways.** Set a filter on `category` in the map's own filter panel and the variable follows at once; type in the picker above and the map's filter follows. A filter on `value` (a numeric column) publishes its histogram brush as the min/max pair ~300 ms after the brush rests — one write per gesture, not one per frame — and consumers read `value BETWEEN '$​valueMin' AND '$​valueMax'`. Both go blank when the filter is removed.\n\n**Click → entity.** Clicking a point publishes its `site` column, for `WHERE site = '$​site'` on any other panel. Clicking empty map clears it — but only a selection this panel published, so a shared link's preset survives. One way only.\n\n**Click → place.** The same click also pins the coordinate, for a spatial query elsewhere: `ST_DWithin(geom, ST_MakePoint($​lng, $​lat)::geography, $​radius)`. Clicking again unpins on the map, but the pair keeps the last spot until the next pin.\n\n**Place → map.** The pair read back: type into Lat/Lng above — or click a row in a table that writes them — and the map centres there at zoom 13. The map's own clicks are recognised and never re-centre it.\n\n**Viewport → bbox.** Pan or zoom, and once the map rests the four edges of what it shows follow. Another panel filters to the view with `WHERE lng BETWEEN $​west AND $​east AND lat BETWEEN $​south AND $​north`. Never put those in this map's own query: its data would shrink to what is on screen, and zooming out could not bring the rows back.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "What the map publishes",
      "type": "text",
      "description": "The variable names in the left column and in the SQL examples carry a zero-width space after the dollar sign. Without it Grafana interpolates them — even inside backticks — and the column meant to name the variables shows their values instead."
    }
